    },
    {
      "position": 21,
      "source_id": null,
      "text": "This is a Level 2 grammar point.",
      "html": "This is a Level 2 grammar point.",
      "credits_raw": "Olivier",
      "content_sha256": "4b2d9274bd3649c830265c91880d9bd13c3e605f93573fbae28eebbb8a307b48",
      "original_created_at": null,
      "original_updated_at": null
    },
    {
      "position": 22,
      "source_id": null,
      "text": "の in 食べかけの is a nominalizer, which makes a noun phrase out of 食べかけ(started to eat but not yet finished).",
      "html": "の in 食べかけの is a nominalizer, which makes a noun phrase out of 食べかけ(started to eat but not yet finished).",
      "credits_raw": "bamboo4",
      "content_sha256": "537b4a90b27433558cee42ac296094434d03e901cb2e06470623fc94a8293f8e",
      "original_created_at": null,
      "original_updated_at": null
    },
    {
      "position": 23,
      "source_id": null,
      "text": "I think \"to almost start\" and \"to start (but not finish)\" are just dependent on whether the verb describes a durative action or instantaneous one.\n\nEg.\n\nInstantaneous = hajimakakeru \"just about to start\" ('started to [but not finished] start' is clumsy)",
      "html": "I think \"to almost start\" and \"to start (but not finish)\" are just dependent on whether the verb describes a durative action or instantaneous one.<br/><br/>Eg.<br/><br/>Instantaneous = hajimakakeru \"just about to start\" ('started to [but not finished] start' is clumsy)",
      "credits_raw": "johnk",
      "content_sha256": "2deffb0a60941ba9cffad0a5edc0d597466b1d165a19168b34dee7d4a8b4c344",
      "original_created_at": null,
      "original_updated_at": null
    },
    {
      "position": 24,
      "source_id": null,
      "text": "So can we say that かけた is similar to ところだ(った) pattern?",
      "html": "So can we say that かけた is similar to ところだ(った) pattern?",
